  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes major application areas of advanced additive manufacturing or bioprinting across three broad categories. On the left, it shows hard and conventional materials such as ceramics, glass, and polymers being used to make medical and optical components, including ceramic implants, polymeric parts, and lens-like optical elements. In the center, the emphasis shifts to soft robotic systems and mobile structures, with examples such as fluidically actuated devices, articulated soft grippers, temperature- or stimulus-responsive “4D printing,” and architected metamaterial designs. These images suggest materials and structures that can bend, deform, or change shape after fabrication.\n\nOn the right, the figure highlights living-material applications. These include biological implants, biomolecule patterning inside a cylindrical setup, microfluidic or chip-like platforms for drug testing and disease modelling, and engineered living materials containing cells or biological components. Taken together, the figure conveys a progression from inert structural devices to dynamic soft machines and finally to biologically integrated systems. The overall message is that the same manufacturing approach can span very different material classes and functions, from rigid implants and optics to responsive robots and cell-based biomedical platforms.",
